<pdd-reason>The one-vote-per-user upvote endpoint. The vote row is the durable truth; the count column is a cache of it.</pdd-reason>

<pdd-interface>
{"type":"module","module":{"functions":[
  {"name":"router","signature":"router: APIRouter","returns":"prefix /api/features, tag votes"},
  {"name":"upvote_feature","signature":"POST /api/features/{id}/upvote","returns":"200 {feature_id, upvotes} | 404 | 409 | 422"}
]}}
</pdd-interface>

<pdd-dependency>prompts/backend/deps_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>prompts/shared/constants_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>

Implement `backend/routes/votes.py`. Requires an identified caller via `get_current_user_id`.

<responsibility>
Record one vote per user per feature, and report the resulting count.
</responsibility>

<non_responsibilities>
This module DOES NOT push the new count to viewers — Supabase Realtime fans out the row change.
This module DOES NOT support unvoting; no DELETE route exists in the frozen `openapi.yaml`.
This module DOES NOT decide what gets built; it only records demand.
</non_responsibilities>

<vocabulary>
**Vote row** — a row in `feature_votes`. Its existence is the authoritative answer to "has this
user voted for this feature", enforced by `unique (feature_id, user_id)`.
**Count** — `feature_requests.upvotes`. A denormalised cache of the vote rows, maintained by this
endpoint. It can lag; it is never the source of truth.
</vocabulary>

<capabilities>
- MAY read `feature_requests` and insert into `feature_votes`.
- MAY increment `feature_requests.upvotes`.
- MUST NOT delete a vote row, call an LLM, or publish to Redis.
</capabilities>

<contract_rules>
R1 (MUST): Return `200` with exactly `{"feature_id": <uuid>, "upvotes": <int>}` — the two fields
required by `openapi.yaml`, and no others.
R2 (MUST): Reject a feature whose `status` is not `VOTING` with `422` and `error.code`
`not_votable`, before any write.
R3 (MUST): Detect a repeat vote by letting the `unique (feature_id, user_id)` constraint fail the
insert, returning `409` with `error.code` `already_voted`. A SELECT-then-INSERT check MUST NOT be
used — two concurrent clicks would both pass it.
R4 (MUST): Insert the vote row **before** incrementing the count. The reverse order counts every
repeat vote that R3 rejects, permanently inflating the total.
R5 (MUST): Increment with a relative update (`upvotes = upvotes + 1`), never a read-then-write —
concurrent votes on the same feature would otherwise lose updates.
R6 (MUST): Once the vote row is inserted, return `200` even when the increment that follows fails —
with a best-effort count read back from the row, and the drift logged at WARNING.
The vote is durably recorded and the unique constraint blocks any retry, so surfacing the
increment failure would strand the caller behind a permanent `409` with no action available.
R7 (MUST NOT): Return or log `user_id`, `author_id`, or any pitch content.
R8 (MUST): Return `404` when the id matches no row.
R9 (MUST NOT): Implement any push, poll, or pub/sub for vote counts.
</contract_rules>

Every error response is the frozen envelope `{"error": {"code", "message"}}` — never FastAPI's
default `{"detail"}`.

<pdd>
Known and accepted for the MVP demo: `schema.sql` is frozen and carries no trigger tying
`feature_requests.upvotes` to `feature_votes` rows, and the Supabase client offers no transaction
spanning the two statements. A crash between R4 and R5 undercounts a feature by one, permanently,
since the voter can never retry past the unique constraint. R6 keeps that invisible to the user
rather than stranding them. Reconciliation is one statement and belongs in end-of-sprint
maintenance (`orchestrator/lifecycle.py`, p20):
  update feature_requests f
     set upvotes = (select count(*) from feature_votes v where v.feature_id = f.id);
Emit a TODO at the R6 branch in the generated code pointing at that reconciliation.
</pdd>

